```{r setup, include = FALSE}
# This file is knitted by vignettes/precompute.R (it runs DeepIMC in Python and the standard methods); the package
# ships the knitted real-curves.Rmd, so building the package runs no code.
knitr::opts_chunk$set(collapse = TRUE, comment = "#>", fig.path = "figures/curves-", dev = "png", dpi = 110,
                      fig.width = 8, fig.height = 4.6, out.width = "100%",
                      cache = TRUE, cache.path = file.path(Sys.getenv("VIGNETTE_CACHE", "cache"), "curves/"))
options(width = 110)
```

A real epidemic curve is not one window with constant parameters: people
change their behaviour, measures are taken and lifted, reporting has a weekly
cycle. This vignette shows how `calibrate_curve()` handles a whole curve:

* **smoothing**: the counts can be smoothed before they are calibrated (by
  default with a centred 7-day moving average) or used as they are;
* **splitting**: the curve can be split where its growth rate changes
  (segmented regression), and each part calibrated on its own, or calibrated
  in one window;
* **recovering the curve**: `recover_curve()` runs the agent-based model again
  from each part's estimates, to show the epidemic the estimates imply against
  the counts.

It uses the real data that come with the package. `vignette("calibrating-windows")`
introduces the five calibration methods, and `vignette("interventions")` uses the
splits to study interventions.

```{r packages, cache = FALSE}
library(epiworldRcalibrate)
```

## The data in the package

Every dataset is loaded with `data()`:

| Dataset | What | Days |
|---|---|---|
| `flu_baltimore_1918` | influenza cases, Baltimore, 1918 | 92 |
| `sars_hongkong_2003` | SARS cases, Hong Kong, 2003 | 107 |
| `sars_toronto_2003` | SARS cases, Toronto, 2003 | 110 |
| `mers_korea_2015` | MERS cases, South Korea, 2015 | 36 |
| `ebola_sierraleone_2014` | Ebola cases, Sierra Leone, 2014-15 | 483 |
| `ebola_kikwit_1995` | Ebola cases, Kikwit, 1995 | 192 |
| `smallpox_kosovo_1972` | smallpox cases, Kosovo, 1972 | 57 |
| `flu_pennsylvania_2009` | influenza cases in a school, Pennsylvania, 2009 | 32 |
| `measles_hagelloch_1861` | measles cases, Hagelloch, 1861 | 87 |
| `covid_admissions_utah` | COVID-19 hospital admissions, Utah, 2020-25 | 1,888 |
| `admissions_utah` | influenza and COVID-19 admissions, Utah, 2020-24 | 1,500 |
| `covid_utah` | COVID-19 cases, Utah, 2020-25 | 1,876 |
| `covid_us_states` | COVID-19 cases, US states, 2020-23 | 1,143 per state |
| `covid_waves_2020` | 27 COVID-19 waves of 2020 | 46 to 153 per wave |
| `interventions` | dated measures of these outbreaks | 117 measures |
| `disease_parameters` | incubation period and serial interval of each disease | |

```{r data}
data(sars_hongkong_2003)
head(sars_hongkong_2003)
```

```{r data-plot, fig.height = 5.5}
show <- list(`Baltimore influenza 1918` = flu_baltimore_1918, `Hong Kong SARS 2003` = sars_hongkong_2003,
             `Toronto SARS 2003` = sars_toronto_2003, `Kikwit Ebola 1995` = ebola_kikwit_1995,
             `Kosovo smallpox 1972` = smallpox_kosovo_1972, `Hagelloch measles 1861` = measles_hagelloch_1861)
op <- par(mfrow = c(2, 3), mar = c(3, 3.5, 2.5, 0.5), las = 1)
for (nm in names(show)) {
  d <- show[[nm]]
  plot(if (is.null(d$date)) d$day else d$date, d$cases, type = "h", col = "#2a78d6", main = nm, xlab = "", ylab = "",
       cex.main = 0.95)
}
par(op)
```

## Known values and the effective population

Besides the counts, the models need the known values of the disease
(`vignette("calibrating-windows")`). `known_values()` gives those of the
paper, from the incubation period and serial interval in
`disease_parameters`:

```{r known}
disease_parameters[, 1:4]
kv <- known_values("SARS", "SEIR")
kv
```

The networks were trained on populations of 5,000 to 10,000 people. As in
the paper, every curve is read as the epidemic of an *effective population* of
10,000: the people who could be reached by the outbreak the counts come from.
A part of a curve with more cases than that cannot be calibrated and is
skipped with a warning.

## Splitting a curve

`split_curve()` fits a negative binomial regression of the daily counts on
time whose slope (the growth rate of the log counts per day) changes at
breakpoints (Muggeo 2003; Kuechenhoff et al. 2021). The number of breakpoints,
0 to 5, is chosen by BIC among the fits whose parts all have at least 15 days.

```{r split}
s <- split_curve(sars_hongkong_2003$cases, sars_hongkong_2003$date)
s
```

`plot()` shows the counts, the regression and the growth rate of each part;
`interventions` marks the known measures (here the ones in the package for
Hong Kong):

```{r split-plot, fig.height = 5}
hk <- subset(interventions, outbreak == "Hong Kong SARS 2003")
hk[, c("date", "measure", "kind")]
par(mar = c(7, 4, 3, 1))
plot(s, interventions = hk, main = "Hong Kong SARS 2003")
```

For counts by date of report, which have a weekly cycle, use
`weekday = TRUE` to add day-of-week effects to the regression.

## Calibrating a curve: with or without smoothing, with or without splitting

`calibrate_curve()` does the split, the smoothing and the calibration. Its
two switches are `split` (`TRUE`, `FALSE`, or the days at which new parts
start) and `smoothing` (`TRUE` or `"ma7"` for the 7-day moving average,
`FALSE` or `"none"` for the raw counts, or the other smoothers of
`smooth_cases()`). Here are the four combinations with DeepIMC:

```{r four-ways}
y <- sars_hongkong_2003$cases; d <- sars_hongkong_2003$date
fits <- list(
  `split, smoothed`     = calibrate_curve(y, d, model = "SEIR", split = TRUE,  smoothing = TRUE,  known = kv),
  `split, raw`          = calibrate_curve(y, d, model = "SEIR", split = TRUE,  smoothing = FALSE, known = kv),
  `one window, smoothed` = calibrate_curve(y, d, model = "SEIR", split = FALSE, smoothing = TRUE,  known = kv),
  `one window, raw`     = calibrate_curve(y, d, model = "SEIR", split = FALSE, smoothing = FALSE, known = kv))
fits$`split, smoothed`
fits$`one window, raw`
```

Each part has its own R0 with a 95% range and its own effective reproduction
number on its first day (`Reff`, R0 times the share still susceptible).

## Recovering the curve

`recover_curve()` runs the agent-based model 500 times over each part, from
the part's estimated starting state with parameters drawn from its
uncertainty. Its median and 95% band are the epidemic the estimates imply; if
the calibration is good, they follow the observed counts.

```{r recover, fig.height = 7.5}
runs <- lapply(fits, recover_curve)
op <- par(mfrow = c(2, 2), mar = c(3, 4, 3, 1))
for (nm in names(runs)) plot(runs[[nm]], main = nm, cex.main = 0.95)
par(op)
```

How close each version comes to the raw counts: the mean absolute error of
the median, the share of days inside the 95% band, and the interval score
(band width plus a penalty for each count outside it; lower is better):

```{r recover-scores}
score_run <- function(r) {
  r <- r[r$what == "fit", ]
  c(MAE = mean(abs(r$median - r$observed)), coverage = coverage(r$observed, r$lower, r$upper),
    interval_score = interval_score(r$observed, r$lower, r$upper))
}
round(t(sapply(runs, score_run)), 2)
```

A single window with constant parameters must describe the rise and the fall
of the outbreak with one R0; the split lets each part have its own.

`run_on = TRUE` also runs each part's fit on over the next part, as if
nothing had changed at the breakpoint; `vignette("interventions")` builds on
this.

```{r run-on, fig.height = 5}
par(mar = c(7, 4, 3, 1))
plot(recover_curve(fits$`split, smoothed`, run_on = TRUE), interventions = hk,
     main = "Each part run on over the next one")
```

## The five methods on the same curve

`method` chooses the calibration method of every part: `"DeepIMC"`,
`"ABC_SMC"`, `"ABC_MCMC"`, `"DE"` or `"NELDERMEAD"`. The standard methods take
minutes per part instead of a fraction of a second.

```{r methods}
methods <- c("DeepIMC", "ABC_SMC", "ABC_MCMC", "DE", "NELDERMEAD")
by_method <- lapply(setNames(methods, methods), function(m)
  calibrate_curve(y, d, model = "SEIR", method = m, known = kv))
tab <- do.call(rbind, lapply(names(by_method), function(m) {
  p <- by_method[[m]]$parts
  data.frame(method = m, part = p$part, from = p$from, to = p$to, R0 = p$R0, R0_lower = p$R0_lower,
             R0_upper = p$R0_upper, seconds = p$seconds)
}))
tab[, c("R0", "R0_lower", "R0_upper")] <- round(tab[, c("R0", "R0_lower", "R0_upper")], 2)
tab$seconds <- signif(tab$seconds, 2)
tab
```

```{r methods-plot, fig.height = 8}
runs_m <- lapply(by_method, recover_curve)
op <- par(mfrow = c(3, 2), mar = c(3, 4, 3, 1))
for (m in names(runs_m)) plot(runs_m[[m]], main = m, cex.main = 0.95)
par(op)
round(t(sapply(runs_m, score_run)), 2)
```

## SEIR or SIR: Utah COVID-19 hospital admissions

The same curve can be calibrated with either model. The Utah COVID-19
admissions of fall and winter 2020 (by day of admission, so no weekly cycle
from reporting):

```{r utah}
u <- subset(covid_admissions_utah, date >= as.Date("2020-09-01") & date <= as.Date("2021-02-28"))
fit_seir <- calibrate_curve(u$admissions, u$date, model = "SEIR", known = known_values("COVID-19", "SEIR"))
fit_sir  <- calibrate_curve(u$admissions, u$date, model = "SIR",  known = known_values("COVID-19", "SIR"))
fit_seir
fit_sir
```

```{r utah-plot, fig.height = 9}
utah <- subset(interventions, outbreak == "Utah COVID-19")
op <- par(mfrow = c(2, 1), mar = c(6.5, 4, 3, 1))
plot(recover_curve(fit_seir), interventions = utah, main = "SEIR")
plot(recover_curve(fit_sir), interventions = utah, main = "SIR")
par(op)
```

## Smoothing options

`smooth_cases()` is the smoother behind `calibrate_curve()`. Besides the
7-day moving average (`"ma7"`) and the raw counts (`"none"`), it offers a
Gaussian kernel with a bandwidth given as a fraction of the series (`0.2`),
chosen by leave-one-out cross-validation of the counts (`"cv"`), or chosen by
calibrating DeepIMC on held-out days (`"calibration"`: the counts are
smoothed from every other day only, calibrated and run, and scored against the
days left out; it takes about two minutes per part).

```{r smoothers, fig.height = 4.6}
yb <- flu_baltimore_1918$cases
sm <- list(`7-day average` = smooth_cases(yb, "ma7"), `kernel, 20%` = smooth_cases(yb, 0.2),
           `kernel, cross-validated` = smooth_cases(yb, "cv"),
           `kernel, chosen by calibration` = smooth_cases(yb, "calibration", model = "SEIR", cores = 4,
                                                          known = known_values("influenza", "SEIR")))
sapply(sm, attr, "bandwidth")
plot(yb, pch = 16, cex = 0.5, xlab = "day", ylab = "daily cases", main = "Baltimore influenza 1918", las = 1, bty = "n")
cols <- c("#52514e", "#2a78d6", "#c0306a", "#eb6834")
for (k in seq_along(sm)) lines(sm[[k]], col = cols[k], lwd = 2)
legend("topright", names(sm), col = cols, lwd = 2, bty = "n", cex = 0.85)
```

The same options go to `calibrate_curve(smoothing = )`, for example
`smoothing = "calibration"` (with `cores` for the model runs) or
`smoothing = 0.2`.

## Summary of the options

| Argument of `calibrate_curve()` | Values |
|---|---|
| `model` | `"SEIR"`, `"SIR"` |
| `method` | `"DeepIMC"`, `"ABC_SMC"`, `"ABC_MCMC"`, `"DE"`, `"NELDERMEAD"` |
| `split` | `TRUE` (segmented regression), `FALSE` (one window), or the first days of the parts |
| `smoothing` | `TRUE`/`"ma7"`, `FALSE`/`"none"`, `"cv"`, `"calibration"`, a fraction such as `0.2` |
| `weekday` | `TRUE` for counts by date of report (day-of-week effects in the split) |
| `known`, or `n`, `recov`, `incub` | the known values, for example `known_values("COVID-19", "SEIR")` |
| `crate` | a contact rate known from other data (optional) |

